.TH TS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ts \- add a timestamp to each line of standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ts
.RB [ \-is ]
.RI [ format ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ts
copies standard input to standard output, writing a timestamp and a
space before each line.
It reads no files; to stamp a file, redirect it to standard input.
This
.B ts
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A line ends at an LF, which is copied with the line.
A line may be of any length.
A last line without an LF is written without one.
The timestamp is taken when the line has been completely read, so a
program that writes its output in bursts gets nearly equal timestamps
within a burst.
Empty input produces no output.
.PP
Apart from console code page conversion, described under NOTES, the
bytes of a line are copied unchanged, including CR and Ctrl-Z, with one
exception.
A NUL byte is not copied.
It ends a line, and what follows it starts a new line with its own
timestamp and no line break before it.
.SS Timestamps
Without
.B \-i
and
.BR \-s ,
the timestamp is the local date and time, by default
.BR "%b %d %H:%M:%S" ,
as in
.BR "Oct 19 21:21:39" .
With either option it is an elapsed time, by default
.BR %H:%M:%S ,
as in
.BR 00:00:03 ;
see
.BR "Elapsed times" .
The
.I format
operand replaces the default.
.SS Formats
In
.IR format ,
each conversion beginning with
.B %
is replaced, and everything else is copied.
Weekday and month names are in English.
These conversions are supported:
.TP
.B %%
A percent sign.
.TP
.BR %a ", " %A
The weekday name, abbreviated to 3 letters or in full.
.TP
.BR %b ", " %B
The month name, abbreviated to 3 letters or in full.
.TP
.B %c
The date and time as
.BR "10/19/26 21:25:19" :
month, day, and two-digit year, then the time.
.TP
.B %d
The day of the month, 01 to 31.
.TP
.B %e
The day of the month, 1 to 31, padded with a space.
.TP
.B %H
The hour, 00 to 23.
.TP
.B %I
The hour, 01 to 12.
.TP
.B %j
The day of the year, 001 to 366.
.TP
.B %m
The month, 01 to 12.
.TP
.B %M
The minute, 00 to 59.
.TP
.B %p
.B AM
or
.BR PM .
.TP
.B %s
Seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.
With
.B \-i
or
.BR \-s ,
the elapsed seconds.
.TP
.B %S
The second, 00 to 59.
.TP
.B %T
The same as
.BR %H:%M:%S .
.TP
.B %U
The week of the year, 00 to 53, with weeks starting on Sunday.
.TP
.B %w
The weekday, 0 to 6, with Sunday as 0.
.TP
.B %W
The week of the year, 00 to 53, with weeks starting on Monday.
.TP
.B %x
The date as
.BR 10/19/26 .
.TP
.B %X
The time as
.BR 21:25:19 .
.TP
.B %y
The year within the century, 00 to 99.
.TP
.B %Y
The year.
.TP
.B %z
The UTC offset as
.BI + hhmm
or
.BI \- hhmm\fR.
.TP
.B %Z
The time zone name.
By default it is the Windows name, such as
.BR "Eastern Daylight Time" .
With
.B TZ
it is the name in
.BR TZ ,
and with
.B \-i
or
.B \-s
it is
.BR UTC .
.PP
A
.B \-
after the
.B %
removes the padding of
.BR %d ,
.BR %H ,
.BR %I ,
.BR %j ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %M ,
.BR %S ,
.BR %U ,
.BR %w ,
.BR %W ,
.BR %y ,
and
.BR %Y ,
so
.B %\-d
is
.B 5
on the fifth.
A
.B #
does the same, and also gives long forms:
.B %#x
is
.BR "Monday, October 19, 2026" ,
and
.B %#c
adds the time.
Before the other conversions
.B #
has no effect, except that
.BR %#e ,
.BR %#s ,
and
.B %#T
are unsupported, and
.B %#z
is the time zone name, as
.BR %Z .
.PP
No other conversion, flag, or field width is supported.
If
.I format
has one, or the timestamp would be longer than 1023 bytes, the
timestamp is empty, and each line begins with only the space, or with
the fractional seconds and the space when there are any.
An empty
.I format
does the same.
.SS Fractional seconds
When
.I format
ends in
.B %.S
or
.BR %.s ,
.B ts
treats the ending as
.B %S
or
.BR %s ,
and then writes a period and the microseconds as 6 digits after the
whole timestamp, even when the timestamp is empty.
So
.B %H:%M:%.S
gives
.BR 21:21:50.644360 ,
and
.B %.s
gives
.BR 1792459310.688698 .
With
.B \-i
or
.BR \-s ,
the digits are the microseconds of the elapsed time.
.B %.\&
is supported only at the end of
.IR format ;
anywhere else it is an unsupported conversion.
.SS Elapsed times
With
.B \-i
or
.BR \-s ,
.B ts
sets
.B TZ
to
.B UTC0
for itself, whatever it was, and formats the elapsed time as if it were
the time since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.
No time elapsed is 00:00:00 on January 1, 1970.
So
.B %H
wraps to 00 every 24 hours,
.B %d
and
.B %j
are 01 and 001 during the first 24 hours, and
.B %Y
is 1970.
Use
.B %s
or
.B %.s
for the total seconds.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-is ,
and may follow the
.I format
operand unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
At most one operand is accepted.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the time since
.B ts
started instead of the time of day.
The count starts just before
.B ts
begins to read its input.
See
.BR "Elapsed times" .
.TP
.B \-i
Print the time since the previous line was read instead of the time of
day.
For the first line it is the time since
.B ts
started.
See
.BR "Elapsed times" .
With
.BR \-s ,
.B \-i
applies and
.B \-s
has no effect.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operand are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A
.I format
with a wildcard is replaced by the names of the files it matches, if
there are any.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
text written to a console is not converted from the ANSI code page to
the console code page, and the console interprets the bytes in its own
code page.
They do not change the names of days and months.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so that a later
.B \-s
is a second operand, which is an error.
.TP
.B TZ
The local time zone for the default timestamp.
With
.B \-i
or
.BR \-s ,
.B ts
sets it to
.B UTC0
for itself.
The format is that of the Microsoft C runtime: a three-letter zone name,
the hours to add to local time to give UTC, optionally followed by
minutes and seconds after colons, and an optional three-letter daylight
saving zone name, as in
.BR EST5EDT ,
.BR JST\-9 ,
or
.BR IST\-5:30 .
The offset is read from the fourth character, so a longer standard name
is misread: in
.B CEST\-2
the zone is
.B CES
at UTC and the daylight saving name is
.BR T\-2 .
A longer daylight saving name is cut to three letters.
Daylight saving time in a
.B TZ
that names it follows the United States rules, whatever the zone.
A value in another form is not rejected but misread in the same way:
.B Europe/Paris
is the zone
.B Eur
at UTC, with a daylight saving zone named
.BR ope ,
and a value beginning with a colon is read like any other.
Only an empty value is ignored, and then the Windows time zone setting
applies, as it does when
.B TZ
is unset.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the input was read to its end, even if it was empty.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or more than one operand, with a usage message on
standard error.
Also out of memory.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed while
.B ts
was writing to it.
.PP
A pipe is noticed only when a write to it fails.
Output is buffered, so
.B ts
can finish with status 0 without noticing one.
A read error is treated as the end of the input, and a write error
other than a closed pipe is not reported.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stamp the output of a build with the time of day:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | ts \(aq%H:%M:%S\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show how long after the start each line arrived:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ (echo a; sleep 1; echo b; sleep 2; echo c) | ts \-s
00:00:00 a
00:00:01 b
00:00:03 c
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the five lines of a build that took longest to appear, with the
seconds each waited:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | ts \-i \(aq%.s\(aq | sort \-rn | head \-n 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write microsecond timestamps with the date:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo hello | ts \(aq%Y\-%m\-%dT%H:%M:%.S\(aq
2026\-10\-19T21:25:19.353488 hello
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stamp in UTC instead of the local time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | TZ=UTC0 ts \(aq%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the
.B ts
of moreutils:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.B \-r
or
.BR \-m ,
only
.B \-i
and
.BR \-s .
.IP \(bu 2
Fractional seconds are only
.B %.S
or
.B %.s
at the end of
.IR format ,
with 6 digits, and there is no
.BR %.T .
.IP \(bu 2
The conversions are only those under
.BR Formats ,
those of the Microsoft C runtime plus a few that busybox adds.
Others that glibc supports, such as
.BR %F ,
.BR %D ,
.BR %R ,
.BR %k ,
.BR %l ,
and
.BR %u ,
make the timestamp empty.
.PP
The timestamp and the line are separated by one space, which cannot be
changed.
.PP
.B ts
never flushes standard output.
When it is a pipe or a file, lines are held until the buffer fills or
.B ts
exits, so a program reading the output sees them late.
The timestamps are not affected.
.PP
The times come from the Windows system clock, not a monotonic clock, so
changing the clock while
.B ts
runs disturbs
.B \-i
and
.BR \-s .
The microseconds are no more precise than that clock.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so CR LF line endings are copied
unchanged, with the timestamp before the line and the CR LF after it.
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
Text typed at a console is converted from the console's input code page
to the ANSI code page, except when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, when it
stays in the console's code page.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
and escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR date (1)
